WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.867
CUDA basics.

2
00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:04.795
You may never write a GPU kernel at work.

3
00:00:04.795 --> 00:00:08.833
But this mental model explains every optimization you have met.

4
00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:12.833
The GPU programming model starts with a kernel.

5
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:16.667
A function executed by thousands of threads in parallel.

6
00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:21.467
Each thread works out its own global index and handles its slice of the data.

7
00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:24.400
Here is how a kernel is launched.

8
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:25.767
You launch a grid.

9
00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:31.800
It is made of thread blocks, up to one thousand twenty four threads each, scheduled independently.

10
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.533
Inside each block, the threads do the work.

11
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:36.933
Now the key insight.

12
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:41.467
Global memory, HBM, is huge but hundreds of cycles away.

13
00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:43.833
Shared memory is fast and tiny.

14
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:46.749
Registers are fastest and smallest.

15
00:00:46.749 --> 00:00:49.589
For ML, the GPU mostly waits for data.

16
00:00:49.589 --> 00:00:51.233
It is bandwidth bound.

17
00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:54.167
Now look back at what you have learned.

18
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:57.700
Operator fusion exists to skip trips to HBM.

19
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.033
FlashAttention recomputes rather than re reading.

20
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:05.367
PagedAttention manages HBM like an operating system.

21
00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:10.000
And speculative decoding spreads one weight stream over several tokens.

22
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.733
Here is a kernel in Python.

23
00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:15.567
Each thread asks for its own global index.

24
00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:19.133
We guard the edge, in case there are more threads than elements.

25
00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:22.733
We compute how many blocks of two fifty six threads we need.

26
00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:24.933
Then one line launches the grid.

27
00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:28.300
Run that vector add at different sizes.

28
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.967
At ten million elements, the GPU crushes NumPy.

29
00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:34.933
At ten thousand, launch overhead makes it lose.

30
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:40.100
Parallelism has a fixed cost of admission, and now you can explain both results.

31
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:42.068
Let's recap.

32
00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:44.500
A kernel runs on thousands of threads.

33
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.200
It is launched as a grid of thread blocks.

34
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.033
Memory, not compute, sets the speed limit.

35
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:52.833
And that explains every trick in this volume.

36
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:55.444
You have climbed all three hundred eight rungs.

37
00:01:55.444 --> 00:01:56.000
Well done.
